findings.body:
`generate_github_mirror_url_from_base` used to call `cdn.set_path(github_url)` before serialising. That call is now gone, so the function returns the bare `TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR` value and the GitHub asset path is dropped. Every GitHub download that goes through `create_agent_and_url` (linuxdeploy, the NSIS/WiX tools, the WebView2 helpers) would request the mirror root, get an HTML page or a 404, and then fail hash verification or zip extraction.

The variable is documented as a GitHub mirror in `crates/tauri-cli/ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLES.md`, and the CHANGELOG attributes it to a China-region use case where this path matters.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="33-40"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The existing unit tests only cover `generate_github_mirror_url_from_template`, so nothing caught this. The base-mirror function needs its own test.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/utils/http_utils.rs" lines="33-40"}
fn generate_github_mirror_url_from_base(github_url: &str) -> Option<String> {
  std::env::var("TAURI_BUNDLER_TOOLS_GITHUB_MIRROR")
    .ok()
    .and_then(|cdn| Url::parse(&cdn).ok())
    .map(|mut cdn| {
      cdn.set_path(github_url);
      cdn.to_string()
    })
}

The PR changes `download_webview2_offline_installer` from `download(url)` to a direct `ureq::get(url).call()`. `ureq::get` uses ureq's default agent, which has the default webpki root store and not `RootCerts::PlatformVerifier`. So on a machine behind a corporate TLS-intercepting proxy, the bootstrapper and the GUID lookup (`webview2_guid_path`, which does use `base_ureq_agent`) succeed, but the offline installer download still fails. That is the exact case the feature is meant to fix.

The original `download(url)` already produces the right agent for non-GitHub URLs, because `create_agent_and_url` falls through to `base_ureq_agent()`. The change was unnecessary.

